1
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:00,890
Good morning, Quark.

2
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,520
Couldn't believe it.
Lisa told Janet

3
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:08,310
that Paul asked Barbara to ask me
if I had a date to the dance.

4
00:03:09,230 --> 00:03:11,860
No. They broke up
for religious differences.

5
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,070
She thought she was God
and he disagreed.

6
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:18,450
Yeah, this afternoon
around five.

7
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:20,910
I'm meeting him at the mall.

8
00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:23,620
I don't know. Maybe. Yeah.

9
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:26,620
Oh, I can't.

10
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:29,000
I've got to hang around awhile
and keep an eye on Nick.

11
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:31,130
He likes to take things apart.

12
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:34,970
Well, that's why I watch it
at your house.

13
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:37,590
Things will cool off after today.

14
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:39,890
Dad has his big "show and tell"
at the lab.

15
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:42,390
Zap!

16
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:47,560
- It works! It really works!
- Nick, get real.

17
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:50,940
The subject... has disappeared.

18
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:52,730
No, no, it's not that.

19
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:54,780
No.

20
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:57,570
Mom and Dad
had an argument last night,

21
00:03:57,660 --> 00:03:59,280
and Mom spent the night at Grandma's.

22
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:01,320
I think she needed a rest.

23
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,370
Right.
Her and me both.

24
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:04,950
Yeah, okay.

25
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:07,250
Okay. Bye.

26
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:15,760
Amy... when's Mom coming home?

27
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:19,300
She's working, Nick.
She'll be home this afternoon.

28
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:24,220
If only Dad could get
that machine to work.

29
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,790
Breakfast.

30
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,670
I need a couple of minutes here.

31
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:00,300
"I'll be down in five minutes."

32
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:03,800
Typing with his gloves on again.

33
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:35,080
Oh, no, not again!

34
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:37,840
Who does he think he is,
Mister Wizard?

35
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:43,890
Szalinski, give it a rest!
It's Saturday!

36
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:47,310
It's construction, honey.

37
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:49,480
You of all people
should understand that.

38
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:50,060
- You're much bigger than he is.
- Szalinski!

39
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:53,560
- Honey, can't we--
- Hold it, Dad.

40
00:05:54,650 --> 00:05:55,440
Don't move.

41
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:57,940
- Ron, what are you doing?
- Defending the backyard, Dad.

42
00:05:58,070 --> 00:05:59,280
That's my fishing rod!

43
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:00,240
Dad, no!

44
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:02,570
Dad!

45
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:06,280
Am I bleeding?

46
00:06:06,410 --> 00:06:08,200
Honey, it's rubber.

47
00:06:08,290 --> 00:06:11,040
- Dad, I told you not to move.
- What's on this thing?

48
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,040
- Super Glue.
- Super Glue?

49
00:06:14,290 --> 00:06:15,960
Gotta mark those trespassers, Dad.

50
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,590
I'm not a trespasser.
I'm your father!

51
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:19,130
Better wash it off quick, hon.

52
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:21,550
- It's taking the skin off.
- I'll talk to you later.

53
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:24,130
- What's with this kid?
- He's 12.

54
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:25,970
Attila the Hun
was 12 once, too, you know.

55
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:27,260
Oh, come on!

56
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:30,310
He's sounding.

57
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:35,020
Weird family.

58
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:51,160
Dad?

59
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:53,660
Look, I finished mine.

60
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:56,330
- Looks just like yours, huh?
- That's great, Nick.

61
00:06:57,460 --> 00:06:59,340
You know, Dad, I was calculating.

62
00:06:59,590 --> 00:07:00,710
If you took all the molecules
in this house--

63
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,090
Listen, Nick, I gotta get this thing
working before I leave.

64
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:04,510
Why don't you help your sister?

65
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:49,300
Stay back, boy.

66
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:50,760
If this thing works,

67
00:07:50,850 --> 00:07:53,430
this'll put us right up there
with the invention of electricity,

68
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,310
the first man in space.

69
00:07:56,390 --> 00:07:57,640
First dog in space.

70
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:08,150
Come on! Shrink!

71
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:09,910
Shrink!

72
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:20,290
Please, please, shrink.

73
00:08:20,790 --> 00:08:21,830
Shrink!

74
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:37,430
On the other hand,

75
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:40,140
we've come up with an interesting way
of making applesauce.

76
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:46,610
You got it, honey.

77
00:08:46,690 --> 00:08:48,240
- Sure?
- Positive.

78
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,410
You're wiping away brain cells,
and there aren't many to spare.

79
00:08:52,530 --> 00:08:54,490
Everybody in my family's a comedian.

80
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:56,330
Everybody except you, hon.

81
00:08:57,620 --> 00:08:58,960
Darn right.

82
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,960
Well, look who's decided
to join the land of the living.

83
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:06,710
And is Russell ready
for his big fishing trip?

84
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:08,720
Not really, Dad.

85
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:10,470
Not really?

86
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:12,510
There's a big fish out there
with your name on it.

87
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:15,930
Fishing's your thing,
Dad, not mine.

88
00:09:18,890 --> 00:09:20,850
- What's the matter with him?
- Shh!

89
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:22,100
What do you mean "shush"?

90
00:09:22,230 --> 00:09:24,940
I see nothing wrong
with wanting to take my son fishing.

91
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:27,520
Russel, I shouldn't
be telling you this,

92
00:09:27,610 --> 00:09:29,490
but he was cut
from the football team yesterday.

93
00:09:29,570 --> 00:09:30,990
- What?
- Honey, please!

94
00:09:31,070 --> 00:09:32,860
What he doesn't need right now
is to hear from you.

95
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:33,870
He needs something.

96
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:35,780
- When I was his age, I was cap--
- Captain of the team.

97
00:09:35,870 --> 00:09:37,660
And if my father wanted me
to go on a fishing trip,

98
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:38,870
- I would've been--
- Been grateful.

99
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:40,960
Russ, he is too small for football.

100
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:43,420
- He is not too small for football!
- Too small!

101
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:45,710
Shh! Honey, when you say
things like that,

102
00:09:45,790 --> 00:09:47,040
you make him feel about this big.

103
00:09:47,130 --> 00:09:50,050
If he wants to feel big,
he should act big.

104
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:56,600
Elbows up.

105
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:01,770
What's the matter with you?

106
00:10:01,850 --> 00:10:03,060
I am on a special diet.

107
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,310
- No toxic waste.
- Fine.

108
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:09,270
Here, Quark.

109
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:15,490
Eat it, Dad, before it gets cold.

110
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:23,160
Mmm, did you get the machine to work?

111
00:10:24,330 --> 00:10:25,380
Yeah.

112
00:10:25,580 --> 00:10:27,170
A few more bugs to get out, but...

113
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:29,130
What about the conference?

114
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:33,510
Jeez, the conference.
I gotta get going.

115
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:37,550
Dad! Dad, wait.

116
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:38,970
Dad!

117
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:41,350
If Mom calls, tell her
I'll be home by lunchtime.

118
00:10:41,430 --> 00:10:43,270
Dad, don't forget my dress
from the cleaners,

119
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:45,690
- Nick's allergy pills--
- It's all in my head. Don't worry.

120
00:10:46,150 --> 00:10:47,230
Wish me luck!

121
00:10:47,770 --> 00:10:49,360
I need luck getting out
of this door.

122
00:10:56,070 --> 00:10:57,370
House of Insanity.

123
00:10:58,160 --> 00:10:59,910
- Hi, honey.
<i>- Hi, Mom.</i>

124
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:01,240
Nick, go get Dad.

125
00:11:01,330 --> 00:11:03,250
He's just leaving for the conference.

126
00:11:03,330 --> 00:11:05,620
Dad, it's Mom.

127
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:07,880
- Where?
- On the phone!

128
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:09,250
Oh.

129
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:10,750
How did everything go this morning?

130
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:12,760
Boy, did we miss you.

131
00:11:13,010 --> 00:11:14,340
I missed you too, honey.

132
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,300
Did Paul Tate ask you to the dance?

133
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:17,930
I think he's going to.

134
00:11:18,010 --> 00:11:19,720
I'm gonna meet him
at the mall later today.

135
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:21,510
- Here's Dad.
- Thanks.

136
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:23,430
- Hiya.
<i>- Hi.</i>

137
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:24,310
Nick!

138
00:11:24,430 --> 00:11:26,020
I called to wish you
the luck at the conference.

139
00:11:26,270 --> 00:11:27,310
Look, Diane,

140
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:28,900
if things go well this morning,

141
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:30,900
everything will change around here,
I promise you.

142
00:11:31,150 --> 00:11:33,400
<i>Okay. I have a house to show,</i>

143
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:36,200
and then I'll be home this afternoon
and we'll talk.

144
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:37,280
<i>Good luck today.</i>

145
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,370
Thanks. Bye, honey.

146
00:11:40,490 --> 00:11:42,530
- What time is Mom coming home?
- Later.

147
00:11:42,790 --> 00:11:44,620
Listen, we got to make this place
spick and span. Okay?

148
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:47,080
Nick, I want you to mow the lawn.
Amy...

149
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:51,790
Good luck.

150
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:06,350
I'm helping my dad with this
real important new invention.

151
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,480
So, um...

152
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:11,610
I... I'll let you mow half my yard.

153
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:13,900
No, thanks.

154
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:18,030
Wow! With that?

155
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:19,360
Yeah!

156
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:22,620
And... this.

157
00:12:25,910 --> 00:12:27,960
Oh, it's a remote control!

158
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:31,330
Tell you what.

159
00:12:31,580 --> 00:12:35,380
You throw in... a box of cookies,
I'll let you cut the whole thing.

160
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:39,420
Oh, but I have to go
and meet Newt right now.

161
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:43,010
Uh, could I do it later?

162
00:12:44,050 --> 00:12:45,310
I don't think so,
because, you know,

163
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:47,180
- my dad's gonna come home and...
- Aw, come on!

164
00:12:48,810 --> 00:12:50,350
Okay. Don't be too late.

165
00:12:50,690 --> 00:12:51,900
Okay, thanks.

166
00:12:52,270 --> 00:12:53,570
No problem.

167
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:55,110
See you later!

168
00:13:01,110 --> 00:13:03,990
Nothing like a hard day's work.

169
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:09,580
French fries,

170
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:12,920
Tater sticks,

171
00:13:14,630 --> 00:13:15,790
meat loaf,

172
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,880
veal "parmedjian."

173
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:20,880
Hey, Dad.
Wanna go play some baseball?

174
00:13:21,430 --> 00:13:23,140
Hmm, baseball...

175
00:13:24,550 --> 00:13:25,600
No.

176
00:13:25,970 --> 00:13:27,720
I gotta load the camper, Ron.
I'm busy.

177
00:13:28,850 --> 00:13:30,180
Hey, Russ, pitch for me.

178
00:13:48,410 --> 00:13:49,790
Hey, Szalinski,

179
00:13:50,370 --> 00:13:52,250
ever do anything normal,
like play baseball?

180
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:53,540
Nope.

181
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:56,420
Baseball's for mortals.

182
00:13:56,630 --> 00:13:58,000
Maybe you could be the base.

183
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:00,920
Maybe you could be the mound.

184
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:02,380
Ronald!

185
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:04,010
Lug your stuff.

186
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:06,180
Later, worm.

187
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:15,100
Russell, give me a hand here.

188
00:14:18,690 --> 00:14:21,280
Russell! Give me a hand here.

189
00:14:23,110 --> 00:14:25,200
Dad, it's early.

190
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:28,870
The early worm catches the fish,
Russell.

191
00:14:30,450 --> 00:14:31,500
Remember that.

192
00:14:33,290 --> 00:14:35,920
The Forresters are coming by
after lunch, so come on! Quick march!

193
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:11,330
Weird family.

194
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:33,640
Russell...

195
00:15:35,850 --> 00:15:39,230
I know it seems strange
when you look at me today, but,

196
00:15:39,650 --> 00:15:40,860
when I was your age,

197
00:15:41,150 --> 00:15:43,070
I was not much bigger than you.

198
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:45,820
I know, Dad. You've told me
about a million times.

199
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:47,450
All right, just hear me out.

200
00:15:47,650 --> 00:15:50,490
The point is,
coach put me on these babies.

201
00:15:50,990 --> 00:15:52,120
Know what happened?

202
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:55,540
I put on 20 pounds
of pure, unadulterated,

203
00:15:55,620 --> 00:15:57,870
blitz-the-quarterback and
rip-his-head-off muscle, Russell.

204
00:15:58,830 --> 00:16:00,750
Now, I'll make you a little bet.

205
00:16:01,250 --> 00:16:04,090
If you work out for... three months,

206
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:07,590
you will be throwing farther,
you will be running faster,

207
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:10,720
and you will be hitting harder
than anybody else on that team.

208
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:36,700
Go ahead, go ahead.

209
00:16:38,830 --> 00:16:42,340
Um, Dad, you know
the weights aren't my thing.

210
00:16:44,090 --> 00:16:45,630
Fishing isn't your thing.

211
00:16:46,210 --> 00:16:49,590
Weights aren't your thing.
Just what is your thing?

212
00:16:52,180 --> 00:16:53,430
I don't know,

213
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:55,510
but I'll keep you posted, okay?

214
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:59,270
Fine, until you do,
you're trying these,

215
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,610
and you're coming fishing
with the family. Is that clear?

216
00:17:03,860 --> 00:17:05,900
I can't hear you!

217
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:08,030
Yes, sir.

218
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:10,450
Better.

219
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:12,910
Uh...

220
00:17:13,530 --> 00:17:15,490
lift with your legs,
not with your back.

221
00:17:30,050 --> 00:17:31,880
What am I doing wrong, Spike?

222
00:17:40,430 --> 00:17:42,810
Russell, you're not smoking
a cigarette, are you?

223
00:17:42,890 --> 00:17:44,980
No, dear.
You know I've given that up.

224
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:01,250
- Your stuff all loaded?
- Ready to fire.

225
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:03,500
We've got the best spot on the lake.
You looking forward to it?

226
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:04,620
Tubular!

227
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:08,960
He's only 12 and he's
already thinking about construction.

228
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:12,010
Why can't Russell be more like that?

229
00:18:12,090 --> 00:18:14,090
Oh, honey, just give him a chance.

230
00:18:22,180 --> 00:18:23,600
Ron Thompson at the plate.

231
00:18:23,690 --> 00:18:25,730
Bottom of the 9th. Clutch situation.

232
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:27,360
Here comes the pitch!

233
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:30,940
Oh, he's swinging like a rusty gate!

234
00:18:31,490 --> 00:18:33,610
He pumps once, pumps twice. Oh!

235
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:36,490
He's never been good
on the high outside pitch.

236
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:39,290
Here it comes.
The fastball is bread and butter.

237
00:18:39,910 --> 00:18:40,910
Grand...

238
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:45,040
slam.

239
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:51,170
Hey, hey, I didn't do it.

240
00:18:51,510 --> 00:18:53,010
- Come on, dope.
- Nobody has to know.

241
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:54,260
Nobody saw it but you!

242
00:18:55,010 --> 00:18:58,140
Let's negotiate.
I'm your brother, Russ.

243
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:00,140
You're not supposed
to fink on your own bother!

244
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:04,390
Turn me in, and I'll tell all what
you spend your allowance on!

245
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:05,770
Come on, Russ.

246
00:19:07,730 --> 00:19:10,530
Look, either you tell 'em,
or I tell 'em, okay?

247
00:19:12,190 --> 00:19:14,110
Okay, you tell 'em.

248
00:19:30,750 --> 00:19:32,260
Aren't you in the wrong yard?

249
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:34,010
- Listen, nerd face...
- Tell 'em!

250
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:35,470
What's going on?

251
00:19:36,010 --> 00:19:37,840
Hi. Um...

252
00:19:38,890 --> 00:19:40,850
I'm Russ Thompson from next door.

253
00:19:41,890 --> 00:19:42,850
Um...

254
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:45,350
Um, my brother has
something to tell you.

255
00:19:45,850 --> 00:19:47,020
Or else I can tell her.

256
00:19:47,190 --> 00:19:48,270
Okay.

257
00:19:48,730 --> 00:19:50,360
See, I was playing
with my ball, right?

258
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:53,480
Well, actually, it's not my ball
exactly. It's Charlie Sudzen's...

259
00:19:53,570 --> 00:19:54,570
Just tell her what you did.

260
00:19:54,780 --> 00:19:57,240
It never would have happened
if their house wasn't so close.

261
00:19:58,110 --> 00:19:59,780
He hit a baseball
through your attic window.

262
00:19:59,910 --> 00:20:00,830
He what?

263
00:20:00,910 --> 00:20:03,910
It shouldn't have been closed
in the first place. It's a nice day!

264
00:20:03,990 --> 00:20:06,040
We'll get it fixed, okay? We'll take
it out of his allowance, all right?

265
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:07,750
My allowance? Dream on.

266
00:20:07,830 --> 00:20:09,710
Okay. We'll just have
Dad pay for it, huh?

267
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:12,750
We'll take it out of my allowance.
Give me my ball back.

268
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:15,630
Until you pay, no man shall pass.

269
00:20:15,710 --> 00:20:17,670
You've got nothing to say about it,
space boy.

270
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:18,880
Cool it!

271
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:22,430
Nick, take him upstairs
and get him his ball.

272
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:23,970
- And clean up the mess.
- What?

273
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:26,390
Do it, Nick.
I don't have time to mess around.

274
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:29,440
Come on.

275
00:20:29,560 --> 00:20:32,400
This is what's wrong
with the American system of justice.

276
00:20:33,940 --> 00:20:34,980
So...

277
00:20:36,570 --> 00:20:37,940
You like to dance, huh?

278
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:40,490
How do you know?

279
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:43,030
Well...

280
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:44,830
- You could see me?
- No!

281
00:20:44,910 --> 00:20:46,620
No... Yeah, I could...

282
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,040
but I wasn't watching you, I...

283
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,170
Awesome.

284
00:20:54,750 --> 00:20:57,260
So your dad's still in contact
with his home planet.

285
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:11,900
And given that my machine
can substantially reduce the size

286
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:13,980
of bulky payloads...

287
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:16,860
and fuel supplies,

288
00:21:17,150 --> 00:21:19,610
the savings to the space program
would be staggering.

289
00:21:19,900 --> 00:21:21,150
Mr. Szalinski,

290
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:24,370
are you trying to tell me that
suddenly size is no longer relative?

291
00:21:24,660 --> 00:21:26,410
Well, that's right,
Professor Fredrickson,

292
00:21:26,490 --> 00:21:29,370
that all matter is made up of not
only density, but of empty space.

293
00:21:29,450 --> 00:21:31,080
And if we can proportionately reduce

294
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:32,880
the amount of empty space
in any given object,

295
00:21:32,960 --> 00:21:34,840
we can thereby... shrink the object.

296
00:21:34,920 --> 00:21:36,500
Uh-huh. Where's your proof?

297
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:40,010
When Einstein came up
with the atomic bomb,

298
00:21:40,130 --> 00:21:41,680
did they ask him to prove
that it worked?

299
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:44,890
You, Mr. Szalinski,
are hardly Einstein.

300
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:48,350
I picked a name.

301
00:21:48,470 --> 00:21:50,640
You have, however, managed
to shrink one thing.

302
00:21:50,980 --> 00:21:52,690
The size of this audience.

303
00:21:52,980 --> 00:21:55,020
Gentlemen, ladies,
I don't know about you,

304
00:21:55,110 --> 00:21:56,570
but I'm going to lunch.

305
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:06,330
Wayne? Wayne!

306
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:10,370
- Oh, hi, Dr. Brainard.
- Don't take it too hard, Wayne.

307
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:13,330
It will take time
to convince people without proof.

308
00:22:14,120 --> 00:22:16,000
I appreciate it.
Thanks a lot.

309
00:22:17,800 --> 00:22:20,710
By the way, you were right
about the electric flea collar.

310
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:22,630
The extension cord was a bad idea.

311
00:22:23,590 --> 00:22:25,260
What could be taking them so long?

312
00:22:26,890 --> 00:22:28,850
I am not missing the mall today.

313
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:31,180
Nick!

314
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:35,810
- Nick.
- Ron?

315
00:22:35,900 --> 00:22:38,070
If you guys are hiding up here,
it's not funny.

316
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:40,820
Nick, you know what Dad said.

317
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:43,190
You're not supposed
to be playing up here.

318
00:22:58,790 --> 00:23:00,050
Where are we?

319
00:23:13,020 --> 00:23:14,230
Oh, my God. What happened?

320
00:23:14,430 --> 00:23:16,020
- Amy!
- Russ!

321
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:18,020
Amy!

322
00:23:19,650 --> 00:23:20,650
Nicky.

323
00:23:20,730 --> 00:23:21,900
Ron?

324
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:26,320
Nick, what happened?

325
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:28,030
We're all the size of boogers!

326
00:23:31,830 --> 00:23:33,200
Be quiet, Quark!

327
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:37,330
Nick, what did you do?

328
00:23:37,420 --> 00:23:39,920
- Me? It was his ball.
- Shut up, wimp!

329
00:23:39,920 --> 00:23:40,130
Can somebody get that?

330
00:23:40,210 --> 00:23:42,340
- What's that?
- It's Dad's thinking couch.

331
00:23:48,680 --> 00:23:50,510
Amy? Nick?

332
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:51,970
Hello?

333
00:23:56,480 --> 00:23:57,690
It's not for you.

334
00:24:00,690 --> 00:24:02,110
What is it, Quark?

335
00:24:02,190 --> 00:24:04,070
Nick!

336
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:07,950
- Listen.
- Are you guys home?

337
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:10,280
Sounds like Dad. He'll fix us.

338
00:24:10,410 --> 00:24:12,950
Mr. Szalinski, up here!

339
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:14,620
Up here, Dad!

340
00:24:14,790 --> 00:24:16,830
Quick, over there by the door,
okay? Get that side!

341
00:24:17,160 --> 00:24:18,330
Is anybody up there?

342
00:24:18,410 --> 00:24:20,500
Nick, get off and give us a hand!

343
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:24,210
Nick? Amy?

344
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:26,710
Hey, Amy, your date's here.

345
00:24:27,300 --> 00:24:28,590
Oh, gross.

346
00:24:29,010 --> 00:24:30,510
Watch out for the crack!

347
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,680
Hurry up. He's coming!

348
00:24:51,910 --> 00:24:54,990
Right here, Mr. Szalinski.

349
00:24:56,290 --> 00:24:57,700
- Dad!
- Dad!

350
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:09,510
Help, help!

351
00:25:17,430 --> 00:25:18,730
Where's my couch?

352
00:25:24,520 --> 00:25:27,400
- What is he, deaf?
- Don't you get it?

353
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:29,940
We're too small. He can't hear us.

354
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:36,410
Dad!

355
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:43,420
- Move over!
- Ow! Watch out!

356
00:25:57,970 --> 00:25:59,350
Oh, my God.

357
00:26:01,980 --> 00:26:04,230
Reminds me of the backyard.

358
00:26:06,060 --> 00:26:08,480
Something tells me
we're not going fishing this weekend.

359
00:26:08,570 --> 00:26:10,360
That should make you happy.

360
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:12,110
Blow it
out your shorts, Ron.

361
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:13,820
Dad can fix us, right Nick?

362
00:26:14,450 --> 00:26:17,030
I... I think so.

363
00:26:17,580 --> 00:26:19,040
RON; Oh, great.

364
00:26:19,580 --> 00:26:20,950
We just have to get back
to the house.

365
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:22,100
I'm never going back to your house!

366
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:24,830
I'm going home.

367
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:27,210
Come on, Russ.

368
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:33,220
- Russ?
- Tell me the truth.

369
00:26:33,920 --> 00:26:35,050
Can your dad help us?

370
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:37,640
Russ, we're supposed
to go fishing.

371
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:39,810
Yeah, right. How're you going,
as bait?

372
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:42,980
What a witch!

373
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:58,490
Russ, you coming down or what?

374
00:26:59,080 --> 00:27:00,580
Well, we can't stay here.

375
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:04,830
Well, I think we should
stick together.

376
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:08,340
Well, come on.

377
00:27:14,340 --> 00:27:15,420
Nicky!

378
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:39,370
I don't think we're
in Kansas anymore, Toto.

379
00:27:40,660 --> 00:27:43,120
I don't think we're
in the food chain anymore, Dorothy.

380
00:27:50,130 --> 00:27:51,000
Come on, Ron.

381
00:27:51,090 --> 00:27:52,750
- We're gonna go with them.
- What?

382
00:27:53,590 --> 00:27:55,800
You let a girl tell
you what to do? Mmm.

383
00:27:56,840 --> 00:27:59,050
Look, Ron. Down here,
you're gonna listen to me.

384
00:27:59,390 --> 00:28:01,510
From now on, you're gonna do
what I say!

385
00:28:02,390 --> 00:28:03,430
Run!

386
00:28:20,410 --> 00:28:23,490
Great Sir Russell Thompson,
stalking the rogue butterfly.

387
00:28:23,620 --> 00:28:24,950
Knock it off, Ron.

388
00:28:26,250 --> 00:28:29,500
We're now a quarter
of an inch tall, and...

389
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:32,000
64 feet from the house.

390
00:28:32,630 --> 00:28:35,510
That's the equivalent of 3.2 miles.

391
00:28:36,090 --> 00:28:39,010
That's a long way,
even for a man of science.

392
00:28:39,170 --> 00:28:41,510
Nick, I've got six hours
to get home,

393
00:28:41,590 --> 00:28:43,310
get big, and get to the mall.

394
00:28:44,220 --> 00:28:45,520
Now get moving!

395
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:52,360
That butterfly
had a wingspan of 42 feet!

396
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:53,900
Nick!

397
00:29:08,620 --> 00:29:09,620
Hi.

398
00:29:10,460 --> 00:29:11,710
Hi.

399
00:29:13,080 --> 00:29:14,290
- Look, Diane...
- Guess what I saw...

400
00:29:15,800 --> 00:29:17,840
- I sold a house.
- Really? That's great!

401
00:29:17,920 --> 00:29:21,050
Yeah, you know that big estate?
The Boorsteins bought it.

402
00:29:21,130 --> 00:29:23,390
I only had to show it
to them 12 times.

403
00:29:23,890 --> 00:29:25,310
How'd your conference go?

404
00:29:26,060 --> 00:29:27,430
I got a lot of laughs.

405
00:29:27,930 --> 00:29:30,600
- Oh, Wayne.
- I failed, Diane. I'm...

406
00:29:31,190 --> 00:29:34,360
I'm just gonna get my old job back,
if I can get my old job back.

407
00:29:34,610 --> 00:29:36,980
But from now on,
things are gonna be different.

408
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:38,570
Different.

409
00:29:40,110 --> 00:29:41,200
Right.

410
00:29:44,450 --> 00:29:45,580
Where are the kids?

411
00:29:45,870 --> 00:29:48,120
I haven't seen them
since I left this morning.

412
00:29:48,540 --> 00:29:49,870
Did you tell them
I was coming home?

413
00:29:49,950 --> 00:29:51,620
Yeah, that's why
they cleaned things up.

414
00:29:52,460 --> 00:29:53,750
I see.

415
00:29:54,290 --> 00:29:55,960
Well, they're probably
just at the mall.

416
00:29:59,010 --> 00:30:01,970
Want to hear something strange?
My thinking couch is missing.

417
00:30:02,720 --> 00:30:05,260
- The one from the attic?
- Yeah.

418
00:30:14,020 --> 00:30:17,360
Hey, Ron. This is a lot better than
those nature hikes Dad drags...

419
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:22,820
Nick, don't you ever
pick up your toys?

420
00:30:22,900 --> 00:30:24,110
What a wimp!

421
00:30:24,660 --> 00:30:26,530
Been looking for that guy.

422
00:30:30,330 --> 00:30:31,830
It's already one o'clock.

423
00:30:32,450 --> 00:30:33,660
So much for the mall.

424
00:30:34,040 --> 00:30:35,670
What if time shrunk, too?
I mean,

425
00:30:35,750 --> 00:30:39,340
what if it's hours to us, but it's
years to the rest of the world?

426
00:30:39,750 --> 00:30:42,260
Okay, that would be cool.
All my teachers would be retired.

427
00:30:42,460 --> 00:30:45,130
Impossible. Time doesn't exist,
except as a...

428
00:30:45,260 --> 00:30:46,510
No...

429
00:30:46,850 --> 00:30:48,560
What's a river doing
in your yard?

430
00:30:48,850 --> 00:30:50,350
It's not a river, dope.

431
00:30:50,430 --> 00:30:52,980
It could be a stream of dog pee
and it looks like a river to us.

432
00:30:55,100 --> 00:30:56,350
Let's swim it.

433
00:31:01,190 --> 00:31:04,150
- Have a ball, baby.
- I'm not swimming in that.

434
00:31:06,200 --> 00:31:09,990
Hey, if we could get some rope,
we could make a log bridge!

435
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:14,410
If we... If we had some logs.

436
00:31:19,090 --> 00:31:20,130
Quark!

437
00:31:20,210 --> 00:31:21,590
- Your dog?
- Yeah.

438
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:23,130
Quark can't hear us.

439
00:31:23,210 --> 00:31:25,510
Yeah, he can. Dogs have great ears.

440
00:31:25,670 --> 00:31:28,470
If we can get on him, he'll take us
all the way back to the house.

441
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:29,850
Whistle.

442
00:31:43,730 --> 00:31:45,990
- What's wrong?
- He can't whistle.

443
00:31:49,820 --> 00:31:51,620
Let's get higher up.

444
00:31:55,120 --> 00:31:56,540
You know how to whistle.

445
00:31:56,790 --> 00:31:59,040
You just put your lips together
and blow.

446
00:32:09,010 --> 00:32:11,930
From the top of that flower,
we could see over the grass.

447
00:32:13,970 --> 00:32:15,310
Let's climb!

448
00:32:21,110 --> 00:32:23,900
No, Patty, no, no.
There's nothing to worry about.

449
00:32:24,110 --> 00:32:26,570
Just have Amy call me
if you hear from her. Thanks.

450
00:32:28,320 --> 00:32:30,320
Amy's not at Patty's, and Nick's not
at the Koestlers'.

451
00:32:30,410 --> 00:32:32,200
I'm gonna call Betsy.

452
00:32:32,410 --> 00:32:34,660
Well, they've got to be somewhere.
Did you check next door?

453
00:32:34,910 --> 00:32:37,330
At the Thompsons'?
They'd rather be in school!

454
00:32:45,630 --> 00:32:46,920
Nick, be careful!

455
00:32:47,970 --> 00:32:49,220
Yes, Mother.

456
00:32:50,970 --> 00:32:52,180
I'll go up with them.

457
00:33:01,350 --> 00:33:04,190
- There was a bee on me.
- Yeah, right.

458
00:33:08,440 --> 00:33:09,740
Wait a second.

459
00:33:11,780 --> 00:33:13,660
Nick doesn't play baseball.

460
00:33:18,830 --> 00:33:20,210
I just figured it out.

461
00:33:20,540 --> 00:33:23,000
I never woke up this morning.
This is all a bad dream.

462
00:33:23,460 --> 00:33:26,170
- Ron, get up.
- You're just a nightmare!

463
00:33:26,420 --> 00:33:28,420
When I get up,
Dad and I will be going fishing.

464
00:33:28,800 --> 00:33:30,170
I'm warning you.

465
00:33:34,140 --> 00:33:37,390
Okay, Ron... maybe you're right.

466
00:33:38,600 --> 00:33:40,310
Maybe this is just all a bad dream.

467
00:33:41,180 --> 00:33:42,980
But if it is...

468
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:45,360
would this hurt?

469
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:46,730
Get up!

470
00:33:48,230 --> 00:33:50,490
If you were my brother,
I'd put myself up for adoption.

471
00:33:50,570 --> 00:33:53,240
Yeah? I hope your face
ends up on a milk carton.

472
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:05,960
My chair.

473
00:34:09,670 --> 00:34:11,050
My couch.

474
00:34:16,390 --> 00:34:17,600
It works.

475
00:34:26,270 --> 00:34:28,440
Nick? Amy?

476
00:34:29,400 --> 00:34:30,780
Can you hear me?

477
00:34:33,030 --> 00:34:34,110
Nick.

478
00:35:33,420 --> 00:35:34,920
Uh-huh.

479
00:35:47,890 --> 00:35:49,230
Nick?

480
00:35:50,440 --> 00:35:51,690
Nick!

481
00:35:56,150 --> 00:35:58,610
Nick!

482
00:35:59,660 --> 00:36:00,990
Are you okay?

483
00:36:04,330 --> 00:36:07,710
When we crashed, my entire life
flashed before my eyes.

484
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:10,880
It didn't take too long.

485
00:36:15,130 --> 00:36:16,470
I'm scared, Russ.

486
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:18,970
We could be anywhere now.

487
00:36:21,300 --> 00:36:22,760
I think we're still
in your yard.

488
00:36:23,180 --> 00:36:23,970
How do you know?

489
00:36:24,060 --> 00:36:25,890
Any other yard,
the grass would be shorter.

490
00:36:25,970 --> 00:36:28,020
Come on, we gotta find
Ron and your sister.

491
00:36:28,980 --> 00:36:31,650
And then if they don't fry him,
he'll go to jail. Your mother, too.

492
00:36:31,860 --> 00:36:33,190
After all, she's the one
who paid for it.

493
00:36:33,270 --> 00:36:35,730
That makes her an accomplice.
Do you know what it's like in jail?

494
00:36:35,820 --> 00:36:37,990
I am gonna tell you
one more time to shut up!

495
00:36:38,070 --> 00:36:41,320
And then what? And then you'll smack
me? You'll go to jail, too.

496
00:36:41,410 --> 00:36:43,490
I'll tell them
after a big bee ate my brother,

497
00:36:43,660 --> 00:36:44,830
you smacked me around.

498
00:36:45,370 --> 00:36:47,540
No jury in the world
would fail to convict you.

499
00:36:48,200 --> 00:36:49,100
The whole Szalinski family
is gonna be in jail for life!

500
00:36:50,080 --> 00:36:52,630
Look. My dad's machine works.

501
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:55,000
When we get home
and he fixes us all,

502
00:36:55,090 --> 00:36:57,880
we're gonna be so rich,
you'll regret this.

503
00:37:00,470 --> 00:37:03,220
Amy, you know I've
always liked your family.

504
00:37:03,590 --> 00:37:05,180
I mean, your dad's a real nice guy.

505
00:37:05,510 --> 00:37:07,140
He's not as weird
as I thought he was.

506
00:37:07,520 --> 00:37:10,230
I love you and Nick
like my own brother and sister.

507
00:37:30,460 --> 00:37:33,000
It's just that my dad
doesn't understand your dad.

508
00:37:33,120 --> 00:37:35,000
Your dad doesn't understand anything.

509
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:20,170
No, Cathy. I just thought
they might have stopped by.

510
00:38:20,260 --> 00:38:22,550
Okay, listen. If you see 'em,
just send 'em home?

511
00:38:23,050 --> 00:38:25,010
Okay. You have a good weekend.

512
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:30,270
This is getting ridiculous.
Where the hell are those kids?

513
00:38:36,020 --> 00:38:37,440
No.

514
00:38:43,150 --> 00:38:44,070
We had to bring the dog.

515
00:38:44,160 --> 00:38:45,450
I don't wanna hear anymore
about the cat.

516
00:38:45,530 --> 00:38:47,660
It's the Forresters.
What are we gonna tell them?

517
00:38:47,740 --> 00:38:50,740
- How about the truth?
- Mae, what kind of an excuse is that?

518
00:38:51,750 --> 00:38:52,750
Don!

519
00:38:52,830 --> 00:38:54,870
Hey, what's up?

520
00:38:54,100 --> 00:38:56,920
How about catching some...

521
00:38:59,920 --> 00:39:01,960
Good morning, Russell.

522
00:39:02,050 --> 00:39:04,630
- Hi, Gloria.
- Say good morning, Sushi.

523
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:06,140
- Don, I...
- Well, the fishing report

524
00:39:06,220 --> 00:39:09,180
looks stellar. Let's make exhaust
while the sun still shines, huh?

525
00:39:09,260 --> 00:39:11,260
- I called Charlie at Bass Lake.
- I know, but Don...

526
00:39:11,350 --> 00:39:12,850
He says the fish
are jumping on the boat.

527
00:39:12,930 --> 00:39:14,020
Oh! And I brought my new...

528
00:39:14,100 --> 00:39:16,730
Donald, that man over there
is flying.

529
00:39:17,190 --> 00:39:18,150
Right, Gloria.

530
00:39:18,310 --> 00:39:20,780
Don, uh, something's come up.
I don't think we're gonna be able

531
00:39:20,860 --> 00:39:22,650
to make it.
Maybe you ought to go on ahead.

532
00:39:23,070 --> 00:39:24,900
What do... What do you mean
you don't think you're gonna

533
00:39:24,990 --> 00:39:26,610
be able to make it?
We're caravaning, good buddy!

534
00:39:26,700 --> 00:39:29,160
- Donald, I am...
- Not now, Gloria!

535
00:39:29,240 --> 00:39:32,080
And besides, if you're not there
by six, you'll lose your deposit.

536
00:39:32,160 --> 00:39:33,240
Confidentially...

537
00:39:35,080 --> 00:39:36,620
Mae hasn't been feeling too well.

538
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:39,420
Plumbing.

539
00:39:40,750 --> 00:39:41,880
Plumbing?

540
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:46,550
Well, this isn't the big
Russ Thompson I know. I mean,

541
00:39:46,680 --> 00:39:49,510
Plumbing would not stop
the big Russ Thompson I know.

542
00:39:49,590 --> 00:39:51,720
Especially with
an $80 deposit on the line!

543
00:39:51,810 --> 00:39:54,600
Don, hook a big one for me, okay?

544
00:40:05,490 --> 00:40:06,650
Don?

545
00:40:11,370 --> 00:40:12,830
Let's roll, Gloria.

546
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:14,790
- Come on, get in.
- What's wrong with...

547
00:40:15,040 --> 00:40:16,120
Come on.

548
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:21,840
Mm-hmm.

549
00:40:28,880 --> 00:40:30,760
- Did you tell him the truth?
- Honey!

550
00:40:32,010 --> 00:40:33,930
- Did they take it okay?
- Mae.

551
00:40:38,140 --> 00:40:41,440
80 bucks, Mae. Non-refundable!

552
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:43,400
Those kids are grounded!

553
00:40:51,110 --> 00:40:52,200
Ugh...

554
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:54,580
Mud is still mud,
no matter how small you are.

555
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,710
I can't believe some stores
actually charge for this stuff.

556
00:41:03,340 --> 00:41:05,500
That was really great,
what you did for Nick.

557
00:41:06,630 --> 00:41:09,300
What about you, Amy?
He saved you, too.

558
00:41:12,090 --> 00:41:13,640
Yeah, I know, Nick.

559
00:41:16,100 --> 00:41:18,100
Where'd you learn
artificial respiration?

560
00:41:21,020 --> 00:41:23,690
- French class, kid.
- Oh.

561
00:41:25,270 --> 00:41:26,570
French class?

562
00:41:46,250 --> 00:41:47,630
How about a truce, okay?

563
00:41:51,380 --> 00:41:52,510
Okay.

564
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:04,860
I think this is the flagstone
in the middle of the yard.

565
00:42:05,900 --> 00:42:07,230
You mean we're halfway there?

566
00:42:07,570 --> 00:42:08,940
Yeah, I think so.

567
00:42:09,110 --> 00:42:10,360
Let's go!

568
00:42:17,120 --> 00:42:18,450
French class.

569
00:42:33,220 --> 00:42:35,550
I'd die for a strawberry sundae...

570
00:42:35,970 --> 00:42:38,640
with chocolate sprinkles
and a banana split.

571
00:42:40,600 --> 00:42:43,020
I could eat a corn dog
the size of a truck.

572
00:42:44,140 --> 00:42:47,270
Ron, if you had a corn dog,
it would be the size of a truck.

573
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:51,150
I've died and gone to heaven.

574
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:53,070
It's as big as a house!

575
00:42:53,490 --> 00:42:54,820
I saw it first! It's mine!

576
00:42:55,990 --> 00:42:58,120
I got dibs on the cream filling!

577
00:42:58,280 --> 00:42:59,950
Nick, it's one of your cookies!

578
00:43:09,880 --> 00:43:11,800
They're never gonna
believe this at school.

579
00:43:12,670 --> 00:43:13,880
You guys, come on.

580
00:43:14,720 --> 00:43:16,590
Somebody take this cookie!

581
00:43:17,510 --> 00:43:19,390
Nicky, take this thing!

582
00:43:20,510 --> 00:43:22,850
Help! Nicky!

583
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:26,810
Come on, you guys.
This is your ant!

584
00:43:27,650 --> 00:43:29,270
- Nicky, help me!
- She did it.

585
00:43:30,230 --> 00:43:31,690
- Somebody help me!
- You know...

586
00:43:32,530 --> 00:43:34,610
- your sister's not bad...
- Nicky!

587
00:43:34,820 --> 00:43:36,660
- for a girl.
- Of course.

588
00:43:50,500 --> 00:43:53,170
Thank you, officer. We'd appreciate
you sending somebody by.

589
00:43:53,510 --> 00:43:55,510
Oh, okay, it's 646--

590
00:43:59,970 --> 00:44:02,310
That guy's got serious problems.

591
00:44:03,260 --> 00:44:05,100
<i>Mrs. Thompson. Hello?</i>

592
00:44:06,020 --> 00:44:08,940
646 Sycamore. Yes.

593
00:44:12,440 --> 00:44:14,030
Great idea, Russ,

594
00:44:14,190 --> 00:44:16,110
even if the boy genius
didn't think of it.

595
00:44:16,200 --> 00:44:17,820
Drawbridge!

596
00:44:21,280 --> 00:44:23,030
At this rate,
we'll be home by dinner.

597
00:44:24,200 --> 00:44:25,100
Maybe I can still go out...

598
00:44:27,580 --> 00:44:28,830
with Patty.

599
00:44:29,920 --> 00:44:33,460
Well, our weekend's shot.
We'll never get to go camping now.

600
00:44:33,550 --> 00:44:35,840
I know Dad.
We're grounded.

601
00:44:36,260 --> 00:44:38,220
We are camping, Ron. Only better.

602
00:44:38,880 --> 00:44:40,180
This is like a safari.

603
00:44:40,470 --> 00:44:43,640
Hey, yeah. I never thought
of it that way, you know?

604
00:44:44,180 --> 00:44:45,810
Onward!

605
00:45:02,700 --> 00:45:04,080
Amy?

606
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:06,450
Nick?

607
00:45:07,100 --> 00:45:09,250
Wayne?

608
00:45:18,420 --> 00:45:22,050
Yeah. Um, I would like to report,
um, two missing children.

609
00:45:22,140 --> 00:45:24,600
Well, instead of flapping
your gums about it, why don't you go out--

610
00:45:24,680 --> 00:45:26,100
I'm sure
it's nothing to worry about.

611
00:45:26,180 --> 00:45:28,810
This sort of thing happens all the time.
You have a little spat

612
00:45:28,890 --> 00:45:30,390
- with the kids...
- Whoa, we did not have a spat.

613
00:45:30,520 --> 00:45:32,480
You were kind of upset
about Russ quitting the team, hon.

614
00:45:32,560 --> 00:45:34,110
Quit! I thought he was cut!

615
00:45:34,610 --> 00:45:36,440
He was afraid to tell you he quit.

616
00:45:36,530 --> 00:45:38,610
Quit... What do you mean
he was afraid to tell me?

617
00:45:38,690 --> 00:45:40,740
He can tell me anything he wants.
You know me! I always listen!

618
00:45:40,860 --> 00:45:42,110
Uh-huh.

619
00:45:42,990 --> 00:45:44,320
What's "uh-huh" supposed
to mean?

620
00:45:44,410 --> 00:45:45,950
- Russell!
- No. I don't like the way he said it.

621
00:45:46,030 --> 00:45:47,990
You don't need
to get upset, Mr. Thomson.

622
00:45:48,290 --> 00:45:50,040
You know, it's not unusual
for these runaways...

623
00:45:50,120 --> 00:45:53,080
Whoa, my kids
did not run away. They're happy kids,

624
00:45:53,170 --> 00:45:55,340
and there's no way you can make
me believe that they ran away!

625
00:45:56,500 --> 00:45:57,670
Here he goes with his writing again.

626
00:45:57,760 --> 00:45:59,970
- Don't you ever say ever anything?
- Uh-huh.

627
00:46:00,050 --> 00:46:01,720
Russell!

628
00:46:02,300 --> 00:46:04,890
Why do I feel like
I am the one on trial here?

629
00:46:05,010 --> 00:46:07,600
- I can't imagine, dear.
- What do you mean, you can't imagine?

630
00:46:07,680 --> 00:46:10,060
Well, I think we have enough
to get started.

631
00:46:10,270 --> 00:46:13,100
Please, give us a call
if you hear from the boys.

632
00:46:13,350 --> 00:46:14,440
Fine.

633
00:46:22,820 --> 00:46:24,660
Don't they need
a warrant or something?

634
00:46:24,820 --> 00:46:27,370
Russell, there's a few
things about yourself

635
00:46:27,450 --> 00:46:28,740
I think you should know.

636
00:46:29,580 --> 00:46:31,580
Well, like what?

637
00:46:31,830 --> 00:46:33,500
Well, I think you need
to sit down...

638
00:46:36,130 --> 00:46:38,500
Oh, another
missing children report.

639
00:46:39,090 --> 00:46:40,840
644 Sycamore.

640
00:46:42,130 --> 00:46:43,430
That's next door.

641
00:46:44,970 --> 00:46:47,100
I called the police.
What's on your head?

642
00:46:47,300 --> 00:46:49,640
- I was looking for the kids.
- Where, in a coal mine?

643
00:46:49,720 --> 00:46:51,890
- They're in the backyard.
- They are?

644
00:46:51,980 --> 00:46:54,060
Diane, I got something
real important to tell you.

645
00:46:55,020 --> 00:46:56,900
That is the couch from the attic.

646
00:46:58,820 --> 00:47:00,570
You can see the marks
where Quark chewed the arms.

647
00:47:00,650 --> 00:47:02,990
I found it on the floor.
It's my thinking couch.

648
00:47:04,280 --> 00:47:05,450
Wayne...

649
00:47:06,660 --> 00:47:07,990
are you trying to tell me...

650
00:47:09,660 --> 00:47:10,740
you did it?

651
00:47:11,620 --> 00:47:12,750
It works?

652
00:47:13,290 --> 00:47:14,620
The machine works?

653
00:47:15,250 --> 00:47:17,920
- Do the kids know?
- Well, yeah. The kids know.

654
00:47:18,250 --> 00:47:20,090
- That's great.
- It's not that great.

655
00:47:20,250 --> 00:47:21,840
- Why?
- I shrunk the kids.

656
00:47:22,720 --> 00:47:24,130
- What?
- And the Thompson kids, too.

657
00:47:24,220 --> 00:47:25,840
They're about this big.
They're in the backyard.

658
00:47:26,140 --> 00:47:28,050
- What?
- I threw them out with the trash.

659
00:47:28,260 --> 00:47:30,680
Stay here. I'll get the door.

660
00:47:32,680 --> 00:47:33,770
Yes?

661
00:47:34,640 --> 00:47:37,940
Did, uh, you report
some missing children?

662
00:47:38,650 --> 00:47:40,940
Oh, there must be some mistake.
Ours are in the backyard.

663
00:47:41,230 --> 00:47:42,400
Right, honey?

664
00:47:45,700 --> 00:47:46,910
She's...

665
00:47:47,860 --> 00:47:49,160
Thank you!

666
00:48:03,760 --> 00:48:06,800
How are you doing, boy?
How about a pit stop?

667
00:48:13,140 --> 00:48:15,730
- What is it, Ron?
- Antie needs a recharge.

668
00:48:22,610 --> 00:48:23,900
Here, you deserve this.

669
00:48:25,990 --> 00:48:28,070
We're not gonna make it before dark.

670
00:48:29,110 --> 00:48:30,910
Yeah, even if we did,

671
00:48:30,990 --> 00:48:32,370
the mall would be closed.

672
00:48:33,030 --> 00:48:34,540
Don't eat so fast.

673
00:48:34,950 --> 00:48:36,100
Hey... that's my hand.

674
00:48:40,120 --> 00:48:41,500
Why don't we let him go?

675
00:48:41,630 --> 00:48:43,750
He's worn out, and his family
might be worried about him.

676
00:48:44,670 --> 00:48:47,590
- It's okay with me.
- Yeah, me too, I guess.

677
00:48:47,760 --> 00:48:49,220
Help me get off the harness.

678
00:48:59,270 --> 00:49:00,520
You can go now.

679
00:49:05,320 --> 00:49:07,740
Come on. Just start walking.
He'll get the idea.

680
00:49:10,950 --> 00:49:12,410
Thanks for the lift, Antie.

681
00:49:13,280 --> 00:49:15,490
You're free.
You get home now.

682
00:49:16,120 --> 00:49:18,160
Shoo!

683
00:49:20,620 --> 00:49:22,130
Looks like you've got a new pet.

684
00:49:22,580 --> 00:49:24,420
Dumb ant doesn't know
what's best for him.

685
00:49:26,380 --> 00:49:28,380
Go on! Go home!

686
00:49:28,550 --> 00:49:30,130
Mom will never let me keep you.

687
00:49:37,560 --> 00:49:38,680
Amy?

688
00:49:39,060 --> 00:49:40,270
- Nick?
- Nick?

689
00:49:40,480 --> 00:49:42,440
- Are you there, kids?
- Nicky!

690
00:49:43,440 --> 00:49:44,860
Where are you?

691
00:49:47,150 --> 00:49:49,110
- Any luck?
- No.

692
00:50:05,710 --> 00:50:06,800
Nick?

693
00:50:08,420 --> 00:50:09,470
Amy?

694
00:50:13,680 --> 00:50:15,010
Do you see anything yet?

695
00:50:15,930 --> 00:50:17,310
It's too thick.

696
00:50:18,390 --> 00:50:20,060
It's supposed to be thick.
It's grass!

697
00:50:21,890 --> 00:50:22,850
Keep looking.

698
00:50:23,390 --> 00:50:25,230
- Nick?
- Amy?

699
00:50:31,740 --> 00:50:33,910
- What was that?
- Ow!

700
00:50:34,450 --> 00:50:36,490
Wa... Watch out!

701
00:50:38,490 --> 00:50:39,700
Come on.

702
00:50:49,170 --> 00:50:51,420
- Ugh...
- Where did that come from?

703
00:50:58,680 --> 00:51:01,220
- Yeah, it's Dad's brand.
- He told us he quit!

704
00:51:05,020 --> 00:51:07,270
Ugh. Looks like he started up again.

705
00:51:08,320 --> 00:51:10,980
No, he only smokes when
he's really worried about something.

706
00:51:13,280 --> 00:51:16,610
Nicky, be careful!

707
00:51:18,570 --> 00:51:20,870
Hey. Hey, maybe we can use this.

708
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:39,510
Cool! Now, we can see in the dark!

709
00:51:45,600 --> 00:51:48,310
Hey, Antie, look! Headlights!

710
00:51:49,480 --> 00:51:51,020
I didn't force him
to join the team.

711
00:51:51,110 --> 00:51:54,490
I know, but you always
make such a big deal out of sports!

712
00:51:54,650 --> 00:51:55,780
Because sports build character.

713
00:51:55,860 --> 00:51:57,610
No, they don't.
They build muscle!

714
00:51:58,530 --> 00:52:00,160
Russell, you've got to understand...

715
00:52:01,120 --> 00:52:02,030
Wayne?

716
00:52:02,120 --> 00:52:04,160
Yeah?

717
00:52:04,100 --> 00:52:06,580
We've got to tell the Thompsons.

718
00:52:17,760 --> 00:52:20,010
What do you take me for?
A complete idiot?

719
00:52:21,180 --> 00:52:23,180
- How did this happen?
- Hold on a second, Mae.

720
00:52:23,510 --> 00:52:24,680
I'll handle it, all right?

721
00:52:25,730 --> 00:52:26,680
How did this happen?

722
00:52:26,770 --> 00:52:28,600
Well, the machine analyzes
the molecular structure

723
00:52:28,690 --> 00:52:31,150
of each compound, and then
through laser inversion, it takes...

724
00:52:31,230 --> 00:52:34,480
Wait, Wayne, wait. I think
the Thompsons need to see the couch.

725
00:52:36,700 --> 00:52:38,860
This guy is a waste of skin.

726
00:52:40,820 --> 00:52:42,870
- This is a microscope.
- I know.

727
00:52:44,790 --> 00:52:45,100
Look in there.

728
00:52:47,250 --> 00:52:48,370
All right.

729
00:52:49,710 --> 00:52:52,170
You see, that's the couch that used
to be sitting right over here

730
00:52:52,250 --> 00:52:53,460
until the machine shrunk it.

731
00:52:53,550 --> 00:52:55,170
All right,
so there's some doll furniture.

732
00:52:55,300 --> 00:52:57,670
Big deal. They sell
that kind of stuff at swap meets!

733
00:52:58,880 --> 00:53:00,390
Why didn't you tell us earlier?

734
00:53:00,930 --> 00:53:03,890
Well, until now,
the machine just blew things up.

735
00:53:04,100 --> 00:53:05,970
- Are you saying that machine blew...
- Blew up my kids?

736
00:53:06,060 --> 00:53:07,430
- No, no, no.
- No, no, no.

737
00:53:07,520 --> 00:53:10,270
If the machine had blown up the kids,
there'd be pieces of them everywhere.

738
00:53:10,350 --> 00:53:11,440
Wayne!

739
00:53:12,310 --> 00:53:13,400
Sorry, I...

740
00:53:13,770 --> 00:53:16,320
Look, I'm positive about this, okay?

741
00:53:16,690 --> 00:53:18,030
The machine shrunk our kids.

742
00:53:18,200 --> 00:53:19,700
You're the one who needs
a shrink, Szalinski!

743
00:53:19,780 --> 00:53:21,700
- Russell!
- You are a nutcase.

744
00:53:21,780 --> 00:53:25,530
And I'll tell you something.
I have got an air hammer in my attic.

745
00:53:25,700 --> 00:53:27,240
And if you did do something
to my kids,

746
00:53:27,330 --> 00:53:29,160
there's gonna be pieces of you
all over the neighborhood!

747
00:53:29,330 --> 00:53:30,410
Come on, honey.

748
00:53:36,840 --> 00:53:39,340
I think that went well.

749
00:53:40,300 --> 00:53:42,140
I think we should have them
over more often.

750
00:53:43,010 --> 00:53:44,890
Yeah, well,
it's a priority for me.

751
00:53:45,470 --> 00:53:47,180
Now why don't you do
what you're paid to do?

752
00:53:49,140 --> 00:53:50,940
Find my kids.

753
00:54:03,280 --> 00:54:04,570
I thought you didn't believe him.

754
00:54:06,410 --> 00:54:07,580
Well, I don't.

755
00:54:09,580 --> 00:54:10,750
Electricity's cheap.

756
00:54:35,440 --> 00:54:36,650
Come on!

757
00:54:37,020 --> 00:54:39,820
Look at this!
We don't have to sleep on the ground.

758
00:54:43,610 --> 00:54:45,780
- What is it?
- One of my legos.

759
00:54:46,910 --> 00:54:48,200
Give me a boost!

760
00:54:52,750 --> 00:54:54,040
Hey...

761
00:54:55,540 --> 00:54:56,710
not bad.

762
00:54:58,960 --> 00:54:59,960
Yeah.

763
00:55:00,050 --> 00:55:01,840
I don't even have to brush my teeth!

764
00:55:03,550 --> 00:55:04,590
Quit pushing!

765
00:55:04,890 --> 00:55:07,260
- Move over!
- You move over!

766
00:55:08,390 --> 00:55:10,600
- Why don't you take that one?
- Are you sure?

767
00:55:11,560 --> 00:55:13,980
Yeah. I'd rather sleep down here.

768
00:55:20,690 --> 00:55:21,740
Night, Antie.

769
00:55:26,490 --> 00:55:27,620
Think they'll be warm enough?

770
00:55:28,700 --> 00:55:30,160
They look fine to me.

771
00:55:42,880 --> 00:55:43,920
You think you'll be warm enough?

772
00:55:43,920 --> 00:55:44,420
I'm sorry.

773
00:55:46,180 --> 00:55:47,760
That's not what I meant.

774
00:55:49,220 --> 00:55:51,850
You know,
it's not important...

775
00:55:52,720 --> 00:55:54,350
if I sell another house,

776
00:55:55,480 --> 00:55:57,940
or if you get a grant
this year or next.

777
00:56:00,150 --> 00:56:02,530
We've just got to get
this family back together.

778
00:56:05,990 --> 00:56:07,610
I feel the same way.

779
00:56:17,540 --> 00:56:20,130
I gotta fix the machine.
You should get some sleep.

780
00:56:20,790 --> 00:56:23,130
We gotta get up early and come
out here and look for them.

781
00:56:24,590 --> 00:56:25,970
No, I couldn't sleep.

782
00:56:27,800 --> 00:56:29,680
Not with my babies
out here all alone.

783
00:56:30,930 --> 00:56:33,510
Ah, don't worry about them. They got
the Thompson kids with them.

784
00:56:33,930 --> 00:56:35,140
They'll be fine.

785
00:56:36,930 --> 00:56:38,770
That's another thing
I'm worried about.

786
00:56:39,690 --> 00:56:41,980
Amy in the dark...

787
00:56:42,570 --> 00:56:44,030
with little Russ Thompson.

788
00:56:44,570 --> 00:56:45,860
Get some rest.

789
00:56:52,410 --> 00:56:54,290
They'd better behave themselves.

790
00:56:59,330 --> 00:57:00,670
It's funny.

791
00:57:01,540 --> 00:57:04,130
The moon looks the same size
whether you're big or small.

792
00:57:04,590 --> 00:57:07,130
Yeah. Tell that to my dad.

793
00:57:09,180 --> 00:57:11,140
Why? Does he pick on you
about your size?

794
00:57:12,680 --> 00:57:14,430
The bigger the guy,
the bigger the moon.

795
00:57:16,020 --> 00:57:17,520
It's just the way he thinks.

796
00:57:18,680 --> 00:57:19,640
Russ?

797
00:57:21,400 --> 00:57:23,650
- Yeah?
- How come you never came over before?

798
00:57:25,900 --> 00:57:26,990
Well...

799
00:57:31,070 --> 00:57:32,160
I mean...

800
00:57:32,870 --> 00:57:35,030
you talked to me
once or twice last year.

801
00:57:36,410 --> 00:57:39,330
I mean, I would've come by.
I wanted to, but...

802
00:57:41,830 --> 00:57:43,840
I guess I always thought
you were too popular to notice me.

803
00:57:45,500 --> 00:57:47,380
I was too popular to notice.

804
00:57:49,420 --> 00:57:50,630
I was stupid.

805
00:58:19,080 --> 00:58:21,620
Nicky!

806
00:58:23,120 --> 00:58:23,210
Look, it's the rest of them.

807
00:58:26,290 --> 00:58:27,920
I'm gonna go get the Thompsons.

808
00:58:28,170 --> 00:58:29,260
Hang on, kids!

809
00:58:31,470 --> 00:58:33,890
It obviously worked on the kids.
I just don't get it.

810
00:58:35,680 --> 00:58:37,430
Well, you better get it, Szalinski.

811
00:58:37,510 --> 00:58:39,390
Russ, you're not helping.

812
00:58:39,470 --> 00:58:42,100
- So, I'm not helping.
- Russell, you got to understand.

813
00:58:43,190 --> 00:58:44,690
Baseball!

814
00:58:44,770 --> 00:58:46,650
Nick's trying to say something.

815
00:58:47,190 --> 00:58:48,690
I can't make it out.

816
00:58:51,450 --> 00:58:53,530
The baseball, Dad!

817
00:58:54,200 --> 00:58:55,990
What are you saying, Nick?

818
00:59:00,290 --> 00:59:01,660
Swinging?

819
00:59:01,910 --> 00:59:04,620
- Sounds like--
- It's semaphore, Mae.

820
00:59:04,710 --> 00:59:07,130
Wait! Uh...uh, flapping!

821
00:59:07,590 --> 00:59:08,880
- Pitching.
- Um...

822
00:59:09,130 --> 00:59:11,090
- Throwing.
- You're out! You're out!

823
00:59:11,630 --> 00:59:12,760
Baseball!

824
00:59:13,050 --> 00:59:14,130
- Yes!
- Right!

825
00:59:14,260 --> 00:59:15,800
- The baseball!
- The ball!

826
00:59:16,050 --> 00:59:17,260
Baseball?

827
00:59:17,850 --> 00:59:19,220
Good, honey.

828
00:59:22,100 --> 00:59:24,480
Holy smokes. It's the laser!

829
00:59:26,440 --> 00:59:28,570
I got it.
The ball came through the window,

830
00:59:28,940 --> 00:59:30,360
and activated the machine,

831
00:59:31,190 --> 00:59:32,860
and got in the path of the laser.

832
00:59:33,490 --> 00:59:34,910
Why didn't I think of this before?

833
00:59:35,360 --> 00:59:37,200
- I could take a wild guess.
- Shh!

834
00:59:37,280 --> 00:59:39,410
It's creating too much heat
That's way everything was blowing up!

835
00:59:39,700 --> 00:59:42,200
- Thanks, Nick!
- All right, Nicky!

836
00:59:42,290 --> 00:59:43,660
Diane, you take the spoon.
I'll get the machine...

837
00:59:43,750 --> 00:59:46,290
Hold it, Szalinski.
This gizmo's been blowing up fruit.

838
00:59:46,370 --> 00:59:48,880
And you're not trying it on my kids
until you try is on something living!

839
00:59:53,170 --> 00:59:55,010
You know something?
Maybe I should do it on myself.

840
00:59:55,090 --> 00:59:56,890
I'll show you how to
work the machine. It's not that hard.

841
00:59:56,970 --> 00:59:58,890
No, no!

842
00:59:59,090 --> 01:00:02,060
- Do it on me.
- Are you sure, honey?

843
01:00:07,520 --> 01:00:08,940
How'd those get there?

844
01:00:24,870 --> 01:00:26,040
Okay, Szalinski,

845
01:00:26,830 --> 01:00:27,750
shoot.

846
01:00:28,540 --> 01:00:30,380
- No reason this shouldn't work now.
- Huh?

847
01:00:45,470 --> 01:00:46,770
Stand back!

848
01:00:46,890 --> 01:00:50,980
- Honey, you're so brave.
- Come on, Mae. Come on. Come.

849
01:01:04,740 --> 01:01:07,750
- Russ, are you okay?
- Yeah, why?

850
01:01:23,680 --> 01:01:24,810
So...

851
01:01:25,720 --> 01:01:26,970
if this works,

852
01:01:27,060 --> 01:01:28,640
do you want to go
to the dance on Friday?

853
01:01:30,060 --> 01:01:31,350
Can you dance?

854
01:01:33,690 --> 01:01:36,400
- Not really.
- I'd love to.

855
01:01:56,500 --> 01:01:57,340
Oh!

856
01:01:57,420 --> 01:01:58,970
- Russ, Ron!
- Dad!

857
01:01:59,050 --> 01:02:02,340
Oh, I knew my boys wouldn't run away.

858
01:02:03,640 --> 01:02:05,970
- Are you and Dad okay?
- Yeah.

859
01:02:06,060 --> 01:02:06,390
- All the way across the yard....
- We're great.

860
01:02:06,510 --> 01:02:09,640
I wanted to go fishing, Dad.
I didn't mean to mess everything up.

861
01:02:09,730 --> 01:02:11,940
- I know, Ronnie. I know.
- Dad?

862
01:02:12,730 --> 01:02:14,110
If you really want me
back on the team...

863
01:02:14,190 --> 01:02:16,440
I don't care about the team.
I care about you!

864
01:02:17,150 --> 01:02:18,650
I'm proud of you.

865
01:02:20,400 --> 01:02:21,530
I need to take a shower!

866
01:02:21,700 --> 01:02:22,950
Szalinski.

867
01:02:32,460 --> 01:02:33,620
Let's eat!

868
01:02:33,710 --> 01:02:35,170
- Honey, make a toast.
- Good idea.

869
01:02:35,250 --> 01:02:36,540
- Oh, great.
- Oh, I love toasts.

870
01:02:36,630 --> 01:02:38,510
To the Thompsons and the Szalinskis,

871
01:02:38,590 --> 01:02:40,090
- and many more dinners together.
- Cheers.

872
01:02:40,170 --> 01:02:41,220
Though I think

873
01:02:41,300 --> 01:02:42,930
we're gonna be having leftovers
for a while.

874
01:02:43,550 --> 01:02:45,510
Wonder what's for dinner.
Right, Russ?

875
01:02:45,840 --> 01:02:47,260
Well, I guess I gotta carve
this thing, huh?

876
01:02:47,350 --> 01:02:49,010
Hey, want to use
my chainsaw?

877
01:02:49,100 --> 01:02:50,680
Who wants bread?

878
01:03:02,450 --> 01:03:03,780
Hey, wait!

879
01:03:04,320 --> 01:03:05,610
I get it!

880
01:03:06,410 --> 01:03:09,200
French class! Ha-ha!
